menu "Habinari protocols"

    comment "Field-bus personalities. These are alternatives, not layers."

    config HABINARI_PROTOCOL_KNX
        bool "KNX TP1"
        default y
        help
            Speak KNX TP1 through the STKNX transceiver, commissioned from ETS.

            This is the bus-powered personality: the device runs entirely off
            the KNX bus terminal with no auxiliary supply.

            Mutually exclusive with the Wi-Fi/MQTT personality, and the build
            enforces it. The TP1 receiver bit-bangs a 104 us bit cell and samples
            at 52 us, against a measured worst-case ISR jitter of 51 us on this
            board — one microsecond of margin. A radio stack in the same image
            spends margin that is not there.

    config HABINARI_PROTOCOL_MODBUS
        bool "Modbus RTU (RS-485)"
        default y
        help
            Expose the room-controller model as Modbus RTU holding, input,
            coil and discrete registers on the SP3485EN transceiver.

            Safe to combine with any other personality: RS-485 is a UART with a
            direction pin and competes with nothing.

            A board out of the box has no address and is silent. It is
            commissioned either from the programming button — which brings it up
            on the commissioning address, exactly as KNX does — or by writing its
            serial number over broadcast, which reaches a device nobody can get
            to. Modbus standardises no addressing mechanism at all; see
            docs/modbus-register-map.md for why this is the shape it is.

    config HABINARI_PROTOCOL_MQTT
        bool "MQTT over Wi-Fi"
        default n
        select HABINARI_REQUIRES_AUX_POWER
        help
            Join a Wi-Fi network and publish the room-controller model as JSON
            over MQTT, with Home Assistant discovery so the device appears as a
            climate entity plus sensors without any manual YAML.

            Needs the 5-30 V auxiliary supply: Wi-Fi transmit peaks well past
            what the KNX bus terminal can deliver. Cannot be combined with the
            KNX personality.

    config HABINARI_REQUIRES_AUX_POWER
        bool
        help
            Set automatically by any personality that cannot run on KNX bus
            power alone. Logged at boot so a field failure to come up on bus
            power reads as a configuration mistake rather than a dead board.

    menu "MQTT personality"
        depends on HABINARI_PROTOCOL_MQTT

        config HABINARI_MQTT_BASE_TOPIC
            string "Base topic prefix"
            default "habinari"
            help
                Topics are <prefix>/<device-id>/... where <device-id> is derived
                from the MAC address, so several boards coexist under one prefix.

        config HABINARI_MQTT_DISCOVERY_PREFIX
            string "Home Assistant discovery prefix"
            default "homeassistant"
            help
                Set empty to publish no discovery configuration at all.

        config HABINARI_MQTT_STATE_INTERVAL_S
            int "State heartbeat interval (seconds)"
            range 1 3600
            default 10
            help
                How often the state document is republished when nothing has
                changed, so a subscriber can tell a quiet room from a dead
                device.

                This is a heartbeat, not a cadence. The device compares each
                document against the last one it sent and publishes as soon as
                anything differs — at up to 1 Hz, the control tick rate — and an
                accepted command always publishes immediately.
    endmenu

endmenu

menu "Habinari service channel"

    comment "Out-of-band configuration. Not a field bus — see oob_service.h."

    config HABINARI_PROGRAMMING_MODE_TIMEOUT_S
        int "Programming mode timeout (seconds, 0 = never)"
        range 0 3600
        default 900
        help
            How long the board stays in programming mode before leaving it by
            itself.

            Programming mode is the one protocol-neutral "this device is
            selected" state, and every commissioning path is gated on it: the
            KNX individual address, BLE pairing, and Modbus re-addressing. A
            device left selected is a device anybody within reach can re-address
            or pair with, so it does not stay selected because somebody walked
            away.

            This does not interrupt an ETS download. Programming mode is only
            needed for the individual-address write; everything after it is
            normally addressed, so a download that outlives the timeout carries
            on unaffected.

            Set to 0 to keep the pre-timeout behaviour, where only a second
            button press or ETS leaves programming mode.

    config HABINARI_OOB_BLE
        bool "BLE commissioning and diagnostics channel"
        depends on !HABINARI_PROTOCOL_KNX
        default n
        select HABINARI_REQUIRES_AUX_POWER
        help
            Expose the device_config registry over a BLE GATT service, so an
            installer with a phone or a laptop can write the settings that no
            field bus can write.

            The device advertises only while it is in programming mode — the
            same button, the same LED and the same state that KNX individual
            addressing and Modbus re-addressing use. There is no second gesture
            and no separate BLE window; outside programming mode the radio is
            silent and the board cannot be found at all.

            The gap it fills is the Wi-Fi one, and it is circular: the SSID,
            passphrase and broker URI decide whether the device has a network at
            all, and nothing on the network can write them because there is no
            network until they are right.

            Modbus has the same shape of problem but solves it on its own bus —
            see docs/modbus-register-map.md. This channel is still the pleasanter
            way to do it from a phone, and the only way to reach a setting that
            no compiled-in personality exposes.

            Not available in a KNX image, and the dependency above is not a
            packaging decision:

              - ETS is already a complete out-of-band channel. It reaches the
                device through the programming button and the individual
                address, so a KNX device never has the circularity this solves,
                and every setting is in the application program already.
              - The bit-banged TP1 receiver samples a 104 us bit cell at 52 us
                against a measured worst-case ISR jitter of 51 us. The BLE
                controller installs its own high-priority interrupts and has
                hard MAC deadlines; it spends margin that is not there.
              - KNX is the bus-powered personality, within about 10 mA at 29 V.
                A BLE transmit burst is not in that budget.

            Costs roughly 430 kB of flash (measured: 349 kB for a Modbus-only
            image against 776 kB with this enabled) plus the controller and host
            RAM.

    menu "BLE channel"
        depends on HABINARI_OOB_BLE

        config HABINARI_OOB_BLE_STATUS_PERIOD_MS
            int "Status notification period (ms)"
            range 250 60000
            default 2000
            help
                How often live readings are notified to a subscribed client.
                Only while somebody is connected and has subscribed, so this
                costs nothing in normal operation.

    endmenu

endmenu

menu "Habinari device secret"

    config HABINARI_ROOT_SECRET_EFUSE_BLOCK
        int "eFuse key block holding the device root secret (0-5 = BLOCK_KEY0..KEY5)"
        range 0 5
        default 5
        help
            Which of the six HMAC-capable eFuse key blocks stores this device's
            256-bit root secret, from which the KNX Data Secure FDSK is derived.

            Keep this at the top of the range: Secure Boot v2 and flash
            encryption allocate key blocks from BLOCK_KEY0 upwards, so a low
            index will eventually collide with them. The firmware refuses to
            provision a block that is already used for another purpose.

    config HABINARI_ROOT_SECRET_BURN
        bool "Burn the device root secret into eFuse (IRREVERSIBLE)"
        default n
        help
            When disabled (the default) the firmware performs the entire
            provisioning path on every boot but writes no fuse: it gathers a
            candidate root secret, runs the RNG sanity gate, and logs the FDSK
            and ETS device certificate the candidate would produce. That is the
            dry run used to check the values on real hardware first.

            Enabling this makes the next boot of an unprovisioned device write
            the secret into the configured key block permanently. The block is
            read- and write-protected by the write, so the secret can never be
            read back afterwards, only used through the HMAC peripheral.

            Devices already commissioned with the legacy NVS-stored tool key
            get a different FDSK once this is enabled, and must be
            re-commissioned in ETS with the newly printed certificate.

    config HABINARI_ROOT_SECRET_LOG_DRY_RUN_SECRET
        bool "Log the candidate root secret during a dry run"
        depends on !HABINARI_ROOT_SECRET_BURN
        default y
        help
            Print the raw 32-byte candidate root secret over the console during
            a dry run, alongside the FDSK and certificate it derives. The
            candidate is discarded either way, so this leaks nothing about a
            provisioned device — but turn it off if the console output is
            logged somewhere that should never carry key-shaped material.

endmenu
